\title{castSDM: Model Response and Interventional Effects for Species Distribution Models}
\description{
\if{html}{\figure{logo.png}{options: style='float: right' alt='logo' width='120'}}

Audits model-based environmental responses in species distribution modelling. Predictor shifts evaluated over a fitted model produce effect sizes, dose-response curves and spatial maps. Variable selection is a two-stage screen whose second stage ranks predictors by the change they cause in the fitted probability when they are shifted while every other predictor is held at its observed value - a g-computation contrast - calibrated against a response-permutation null. The permutation importance of the same forest is reported alongside as a diagnostic; both permutation and additive shifts can leave joint data support. Effect products carry a range diagnostic: the fraction of evaluated rows inside the training quantile box before and after shifting. This does not establish conditional positivity or causal identification. Both sit in a complete workflow - Random Forest, BRT, MaxEnt and GAM learners, nested spatial cross-validation, ensemble prediction, and future climate projection with extrapolation control. The package targets interpretable interventional attribution under stated assumptions. Optional invariant selection uses 'tramicp' with supplied environment groups; its assumptions are not verified by the software. Scenario contrasts for GAMs can use 'marginaleffects'. Outputs do not establish ecological causality or convert presence-background suitability to occurrence probability.
}
